      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for asking.\n\nFixing only the trust-store indexing would make verification work, e.g. one \u003chash\u003e.N symlink per CA all pointing at the same file. The remaining problem is that the bundle would still be a single Certificate object, and that model is wrong for more than lookup:\n\n- Reporting: a Certificate object describes one X.509 certificate (subject, issuer, validity, fingerprint). For a bundle it would describe only the first CA, so the other CAs are trusted but invisible to clients, including their expiry dates.\n- Lifecycle: Delete and Replace act on one object. With a bundle, removing or replacing one CA means dropping the whole file and reinstalling the rest. Replace of a single object with a bundle is now rejected for this reason.\n- Bookkeeping: the certificate limit, the duplicate check and the removal of the subject-hash symlinks are all per object. A bundle of N CAs would count as 1 against the limit, and the extra symlinks would need separate tracking.\n\nI am not claiming DMTF requires this; the Redfish Certificate resource simply models a single certificate and its properties are singular. The change makes the D-Bus objects match what is actually trusted, and it is all-or-nothing: a bundle that exceeds the limit or repeats a certificate is rejected as a whole and leaves nothing installed.\n\nIf you prefer the smaller indexing-only fix, I can drop this, but I think the object model would then misreport what the BMC trusts. I can also add this rationale to the commit message if that helps.",
